WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.833
Advanced RAG.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:08.200
Here are eleven terms that separate someone who built a demo from someone who designed the retrieval system.

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.100
Agentic RAG puts an agent in charge, instead of one fixed retrieve then generate pass.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.733
A question comes in.

5
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:23.133
The agent decides when to retrieve and what to look for, reformulating queries or choosing sources.

6
00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:27.200
It can retrieve again and again, iteratively, until it has enough.

7
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.462
Then it answers.

8
00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:30.567
This is where RAG and agents merge.

9
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:36.633
Self-RAG lets the model grade its own work, using special reflection tokens.

10
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:42.600
It asks itself whether a retrieved chunk was relevant, and whether its answer is actually supported.

11
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.048
If not, it retries.

12
00:00:44.048 --> 00:00:47.433
That makes it a blueprint for self-correcting pipelines.

13
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:51.967
Corrective RAG adds a quality check before generation.

14
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:53.767
First we retrieve chunks.

15
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:56.000
A lightweight evaluator grades them.

16
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.467
If the retrieval is poor, we fall back to a web search or a query rewrite.

17
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:01.773
Then we generate.

18
00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:04.200
It is cheap insurance against garbage in.

19
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.900
Adaptive RAG routes by query complexity.

20
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.367
Every question starts the same way, with a quick judgment of how hard it is.

21
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:15.283
Simple questions are answered directly.

22
00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:18.167
Hard ones get full retrieval, or multi-hop.

23
00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:22.000
That saves latency and tokens on the easy eighty percent.

24
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.967
RAG-Fusion, also called multi-query, works in three steps.

25
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:30.667
First, generate several reformulations of the user query.

26
00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:32.500
Then retrieve for each one.

27
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.377
Finally, fuse the ranked lists with reciprocal rank fusion.

28
00:01:36.377 --> 00:01:39.833
It catches documents that any single phrasing would miss.

29
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.000
Contextual compression filters after retrieval.

30
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.067
Normally, every whole chunk goes into the prompt, noise and all.

31
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:54.400
Here we trim each chunk down to only its relevant sentences, so there are fewer tokens and less noise.

32
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.567
The payoff is better faithfulness.

33
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:03.167
Contextual retrieval is Anthropic's technique for chunks that lose their meaning on their own.

34
00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:09.600
Before embedding, an LLM writes a one-line note on where the chunk fits in the document, and we prepend it.

35
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.833
It costs one cheap pass at index time, and brings big retrieval accuracy gains.

36
00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.667
A normal bi-encoder squeezes a whole text into one vector.

37
00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:25.194
ColBERT embeds every token instead, and matches token to token at query time.

38
00:02:25.194 --> 00:02:27.367
That is called late interaction.

39
00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:32.484
The result is bi-encoder speed approaching cross-encoder quality.

40
00:02:32.484 --> 00:02:35.300
ColBERTv2 and PLAID made it practical.

41
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.333
SPLADE produces learned sparse vectors over the vocabulary.

42
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:45.733
You get the exactness of BM25, keyword style, but with neural smarts.

43
00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:52.933
BGE-M3 outputs dense and sparse together, which makes it a key ingredient of modern hybrid search.

44
00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:57.400
Matryoshka embeddings are named after nesting dolls.

45
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.000
They are trained so the full vector still works for reranking.

46
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:09.510
And a truncated prefix, say the first two hundred fifty six dimensions, works too, so you can store short vectors cheaply.

47
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.067
OpenAI text-embedding-3 supports this.

48
00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:16.833
Embedding model families are mostly names to recognize.

49
00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:19.067
OpenAI text-embedding-3.

50
00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:20.867
BAAI BGE.

51
00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.633
E5 and GTE.

52
00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:25.988
And Jina, Voyage, Cohere Embed, and Nomic.

53
00:03:25.988 --> 00:03:30.133
Check the MTEB leaderboard, then verify on your own data.

54
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:32.154
Let's recap.

55
00:03:32.154 --> 00:03:36.733
Agentic, Self, Corrective, and Adaptive RAG make retrieval smarter.

56
00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:41.967
Fusion, compression, and contextual retrieval put better chunks in front of the model.

57
00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:45.133
ColBERT and SPLADE give richer ways to match.

58
00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:50.833
And Matryoshka embeddings and the model families help you choose and store embeddings wisely.
